    <div class="prose">
      <p>We consider an attacker that aims to disrupt the operation of the VD-RAG system by causing the retriever to retrieve a malicious adversarial image and the generator to output unhelpful responses to user queries.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cards" data-augmented-ui="tl-clip br-clip border">
      <div class="card"><h4>Same-distribution data</h4><p>To achieve this goal, the attacker is assumed to possess a dataset of potential user queries <i>Q</i>, corresponding ground truth answers <i>A</i>, and KB images <i>I</i> from the same distribution as the target RAG system.</p></div>
      <div class="card"><h4>Injecting documents/images</h4><p>Furthermore, the attacker is capable of injecting documents/images into the KB. This could be realized either by an insider that has access to inject and modify enterprise-owned documents, or by an outsider injecting the poisoned documents/images in public domains (KBs are typically crawled from the internet, such as from Wikipedia).</p></div>
      <div class="card"><h4>Orthogonal to malicious prompts</h4><p>Note that this threat model is orthogonal to works that protect against malicious user prompts, as those assume an attacker controls the user input while the KB is trusted, whereas we assume an attacker who can poison the KB.</p></div>
    </div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>In our work, we assume a weak attacker that can only inject one malicious image <i>I′</i> into the KB, such that <i>K′</i> = <i>K</i> ∪ <i>I′</i>, as a single image is sufficient to demonstrate the vulnerability of VD-RAG. Scaling to multiple images would amplify the impact, however, it would reduce the stealthiness of the attack.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="psec" id="definition">
    <h2>Attack Definition</h2>
    <div class="prose"><p>Building upon the work of Zou et al. (2024), a successful RAG poisoning attack must meet two conditions.</p></div>
    <div class="cards" data-augmented-ui="tl-clip br-clip border">
      <div class="card"><h4>Retrieval condition</h4><p>First, the retrieval condition requires that the malicious image is retrieved for the attacker-specified queries.</p></div>
      <div class="card"><h4>Generation condition</h4><p>Second, the generation condition requires that, when present in the context window, the malicious image must cause the generator to output a specific response.</p></div>
    </div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>To compute a malicious image <i>I′</i> that meets the above two conditions, we adopt a gradient-based adversarial example framework, initially proposed against neural network-based image classifiers. In particular, we extend the widely-used PGD optimization algorithm to jointly optimize the image to minimize a multi-objective loss function <i>L</i><sub>RAG</sub> capturing both a retrieval loss <i>L</i><sub>R</sub> and a generation loss <i>L</i><sub>G</sub> as follows:</p>
    </div>
    <div class="op-eq" role="group" aria-label="Equation 1" data-augmented-ui="tl-clip br-clip border">
      <div><span><i>L</i><sub>RAG</sub> = λ<sub>R</sub><i>L</i><sub>R</sub> + λ<sub>G</sub><i>L</i><sub>G</sub>,</span><em>(1)</em></div>
    </div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>where λ<sub>R</sub>, λ<sub>G</sub> are attacker-chosen coefficients controlling the relative weights of the two adversarial objectives. The adversary chooses a subset of positive target queries <i>Q</i><sup>+</sup> ⊆ <i>Q</i> that it wishes to influence; the remaining queries <i>Q</i><sup>−</sup> = <i>Q</i> \ <i>Q</i><sup>+</sup> are termed negative queries. The set of answers <i>A</i> is also divided into malicious answers <i>a</i><sup>+</sup><sub><i>i</i></sub> desired by the attacker for targeted queries <i>q</i><sup>+</sup><sub><i>i</i></sub> ∈ <i>Q</i><sup>+</sup> and benign ground truth answers <i>a</i><sup>−</sup><sub><i>i</i></sub> for <i>q</i><sup>−</sup><sub><i>i</i></sub> ∈ <i>Q</i><sup>−</sup>.</p>
      <p>The retrieval and generation losses are defined as follows:</p>
    </div>
    <div class="op-eq" role="group" aria-label="Equations 2 and 3" data-augmented-ui="tl-clip br-clip border">
      <div><span><i>L</i><sub>R</sub> = Σ<sub><i>i</i>=1…|<i>Q</i><sup>−</sup>|</sub> <i>S</i>(<i>E</i>(<i>q</i><sup>−</sup><sub><i>i</i></sub>), <i>E</i>(<i>I′</i>)) − Σ<sub><i>i</i>=1…|<i>Q</i><sup>+</sup>|</sub> <i>S</i>(<i>E</i>(<i>q</i><sup>+</sup><sub><i>i</i></sub>), <i>E</i>(<i>I′</i>)),</span><em>(2)</em></div>
      <div><span><i>L</i><sub>G</sub> = Σ<sub><i>i</i>=1…|<i>Q</i><sup>+</sup>|</sub> CE(<i>G</i>(<i>q</i><sup>+</sup><sub><i>i</i></sub>, <i>I</i><sub><i>k</i>−1</sub> ∪ <i>I′</i>), <i>a</i><sup>+</sup><sub><i>i</i></sub>) + Σ<sub><i>i</i>=1…|<i>Q</i><sup>−</sup>|</sub> CE(<i>G</i>(<i>q</i><sup>−</sup><sub><i>i</i></sub>, <i>I</i><sub><i>k</i>−1</sub> ∪ <i>I′</i>), <i>a</i><sup>−</sup><sub><i>i</i></sub>),</span><em>(3)</em></div>
    </div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>where <i>S</i>(·) is the similarity measure between the query and the image embeddings, CE(·) is the cross entropy loss, <i>I</i><sub><i>k</i>−1</sub> is a randomly sampled subset, with cardinality |<i>I</i><sub><i>k</i>−1</sub>| = <i>k</i> − 1, of the attacker-owned KB image dataset <i>I</i>. Note that <i>I</i><sub><i>k</i>−1</sub> ∪ <i>I′</i> represents the top-<i>k</i> images retrieved simulated by the attacker.</p>
      <p>To minimize the loss <i>L</i><sub>RAG</sub> we adopt a multi-objective variant of PGD, referred to as MO-PGD, which iteratively updates the adversarial image <i>I′</i> using the following formula:</p>
    </div>
    <div class="op-eq" role="group" aria-label="Equation 4" data-augmented-ui="tl-clip br-clip border">
      <div><span><i>I′</i><sub><i>t</i></sub> = <i>I′</i><sub><i>t</i>−1</sub> + clip<sub>[−ϵ,ϵ]</sub>(α sign(∇<sub><i>I′</i><sub><i>t</i>−1</sub></sub> <i>L</i><sub>RAG</sub>)),</span><em>(4)</em></div>
    </div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>where <i>t</i> ∈ {'{'}1, …, <i>T</i>{'}'} is the iteration index, ϵ is the perturbation budget controlling the attack stealthiness, α is the learning rate, ∇ is the gradient operator, <i>I′</i><sub>0</sub> is a benign image, and <i>I′</i><sub><i>T</i></sub> is the final adversarial image to be injected to the KB.</p>
    </div>

