  <section class="psec" id="knowledge">
    <h2>Variants Based on Attacker Knowledge</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>We examine different levels of attacker knowledge through variants of the previous attack definition, varying from full white-box access to the black-box setting. In the black-box setting the attacker does not know the target models. We investigate three attack variants at increasing levels of difficulty for crafting malicious images: a Prompt-based Attack, a Direct Transfer Attack, and a Model Ensemble Attack.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="explorer">
      <div class="ex-tabs" aria-label="Attacker knowledge variants">
        <div class="ex-group">White-box</div>
        <button class="ex-tab" type="button" aria-controls="ex-wb" aria-selected="true"><span class="hb-code">WB</span>White-box Attack</button>
        <div class="ex-group">Black-box</div>
        <button class="ex-tab" type="button" aria-controls="ex-pr"><span class="hb-code">B1</span>Prompt-based</button>
        <button class="ex-tab" type="button" aria-controls="ex-ct"><span class="hb-code">B2</span>Complete Transfer</button>
        <button class="ex-tab" type="button" aria-controls="ex-cw"><span class="hb-code">B3</span>Component-wise Transfer</button>
        <button class="ex-tab" type="button" aria-controls="ex-in"><span class="hb-code">B4</span>In-set Model Ensemble</button>
        <button class="ex-tab" type="button" aria-controls="ex-out"><span class="hb-code">B5</span>Out-set Model Ensemble</button>
      </div>
      <div>
        <div class="ex-panel" id="ex-wb" data-augmented-ui="tl-clip br-clip border"><h4>White-box Attack</h4>
          <p>In the white-box setting the attacker has full knowledge of and access to the embedding model <i>E</i> and the VLM <i>G</i>. This is the strongest assumption and thus yields the strongest attack. However, it is a practical concern due to potential insider threats and due to the proliferation of high-quality open-source text and multi-modal embedding models and VLMs, and the emergence of techniques to identify models based on their output.</p>
          <dl>
            <dt>Setting I</dt><dd>ASR-R@1 0.40–1.00 (1.00 for every CLIP-L pair); ASR-G<sub>Sim</sub> 0.55–1.00</dd>
            <dt>Universal</dt><dd>ASR-R@1 0.90–0.98 with CLIP-L, 0.00 with ColPali and GME; ASR-G<sub>Sim</sub> 0.44–1.00</dd>
          </dl></div>
        <div class="ex-panel" id="ex-pr" hidden data-augmented-ui="tl-clip br-clip border"><h4>Prompt-based Attack</h4>
          <p>Prompt an off-the-shelf multi-modal generative model, specifically GPT-5 and Gemini-2.5-Flash in this paper, to generate an image with the desired retrieval/generation effect. This style of attack has been studied by several works in RAG poisoning and illustrates the immediate risk posed by any individual able to inject an image into the knowledge base.</p>
          <p>We attribute this partial success to the textual (typographic) elements in those generated images, exploiting the OCR capabilities of both the embedding model and the VLM.</p>
          <dl>
            <dt>Setting I</dt><dd>Gemini: ASR-R@1 0.60–1.00, ASR-G<sub>Sim</sub> 0.40–0.55; GPT-5: ASR-R@1 0.20–0.40, ASR-G<sub>Sim</sub> 0.63–0.89</dd>
            <dt>Universal</dt><dd>ASR-R@1 0.00; ASR-G<sub>Sim</sub> 0.01</dd>
          </dl></div>
        <div class="ex-panel" id="ex-ct" hidden data-augmented-ui="tl-clip br-clip border"><h4>Complete Transfer</h4>
          <p>Optimize an adversarial image against a surrogate model pair (<i>E′</i>, <i>G′</i>) that is likely different from the target (<i>E</i>, <i>G</i>). This attack relies on the well-known transferability property of adversarial examples. Neither component of the surrogate pair matches the target, referred to as Complete Transfer, which measures pure transferability.</p>
          <dl>
            <dt>Setting I</dt><dd>ASR-R@1 0.00, ASR-R@5 0.00; ASR-G<sub>Sim</sub> 0.46</dd>
            <dt>Universal</dt><dd>ASR-R@1 0.00; ASR-G<sub>Sim</sub> −0.01</dd>
          </dl></div>
        <div class="ex-panel" id="ex-cw" hidden data-augmented-ui="tl-clip br-clip border"><h4>Component-wise Transfer</h4>
          <p>Exactly one surrogate component (either <i>E′</i> or <i>G′</i>) matches the target, referred to as Component-wise Transfer, which measures the transferability between individual components.</p>
          <dl>
            <dt>Setting I</dt><dd>Same VLM: ASR-R@1 0.00, ASR-G<sub>Sim</sub> 0.85; Same embedder: ASR-R@1 0.76, ASR-G<sub>Sim</sub> 0.45</dd>
            <dt>Universal</dt><dd>Same VLM: ASR-R@1 0.00, ASR-G<sub>Sim</sub> 0.91; Same embedder: ASR-R@1 0.32, ASR-G<sub>Sim</sub> −0.01</dd>
          </dl></div>
        <div class="ex-panel" id="ex-in" hidden data-augmented-ui="tl-clip br-clip border"><h4>In-set Model Ensemble</h4>
          <p>Optimize the image jointly over all models in a set of surrogate embedding models <i>E′</i> and a set of surrogate VLMs <i>G′</i>. Both surrogate sets contain the target, <i>E</i> ∈ <i>E′</i> ∧ <i>G</i> ∈ <i>G′</i>, referred to as the In-set Model Ensemble case, which assesses the risk of an attack with a representative set.</p>
          <dl>
            <dt>Setting I</dt><dd>ASR-R@1 1.00 (CLIP-L), 0.40 (ColPali), 0.00 (GME); ASR-G<sub>Sim</sub> 0.47–0.81</dd>
            <dt>Universal</dt><dd>ASR-R@1 0.07 (CLIP-L), 0.00 otherwise; ASR-G<sub>Sim</sub> 0.07–0.14, or 0.88–0.90 with Qwen2.5-3B</dd>
          </dl></div>
        <div class="ex-panel" id="ex-out" hidden data-augmented-ui="tl-clip br-clip border"><h4>Out-set Model Ensemble</h4>
          <p>Optimizing over large surrogate sets aims to increase the chance that either: (1) the target models are contained in the surrogate sets, or (2) the resulting image transfers when the target models are not in the surrogate sets. Neither of the surrogate sets contain the target, <i>E</i> ∉ <i>E′</i> ∧ <i>G</i> ∉ <i>G′</i>, referred to as the Out-set Model Ensemble case, which measures the pure transferability of the ensemble-based optimization.</p>
          <dl>
            <dt>Setting I</dt><dd>ASR-R@1 0.00, ASR-R@5 0.00; ASR-G<sub>Sim</sub> 0.47</dd>
            <dt>Universal</dt><dd>ASR-R@1 0.00; ASR-G<sub>Sim</sub> −0.01</dd>
          </dl></div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="footnote">ViDoRe-V1-AI, mean values. ASR-G<sub>Sim</sub> is ASR-G<sub>Sim</sub>@−1.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="psec" id="setup">
    <h2>Experiment Design</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>We evaluate the attacks on two visual document retrieval datasets taken from the ViDoRe benchmark versions 1 and 2. We use a mix of embedding models that range in size, recency, and target applications.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="table-wrap"><table class="data-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Component</th><th>Name</th><th>Details</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Dataset</td><td>ViDoRe-V1-AI</td><td>100 queries and 1000 images (with exactly one relevant ground-truth image in the KB per query)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Dataset</td><td>ViDoRe-V2-ESG</td><td>52 queries and 1538 images with an average of 2.5 relevant images per query</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Embedder</td><td>CLIP-ViT-LARGE (CLIP-L)</td><td>0.4B; trained using contrastive learning to achieve zero-shot image classification; not specifically trained for VDR; 7.2 million monthly downloads</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Embedder</td><td>GME-Qwen2-VL-2B (GME)</td><td>2.2B; fine-tuned from Qwen2-2B-VL on several tasks, including VDR; 30th on the ViDoRe benchmark</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Embedder</td><td>ColPali-v1.3 (ColPali)</td><td>3B; state-of-the-art in visual document retrieval; ColBERT-style late embedding interaction with MaxSim; 37th on the ViDoRe benchmark</td></tr>
        <tr><td>VLM</td><td>SmolVLM-Instruct</td><td>2.2B; 34th on OpenCompass (open-source, under 4B)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>VLM</td><td>Qwen2.5-VL-3B-Instruct</td><td>3.75B; 7th on OpenCompass (open-source, under 4B)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>VLM</td><td>InternVL3-2B</td><td>2B; 8th on OpenCompass (open-source, under 4B)</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table></div>
    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:20px">
      <p>We split the queries of each dataset into a set used to optimize the malicious image (80%) and a set to evaluate the attack for the universal objective (20%). For all the above models, unless otherwise stated, we assume the retriever only retrieves the top-1 relevant image from the KB.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Evaluation Metrics</h3>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>We evaluate the RAG system and each attack using the following performance metrics for retrieval, where ↑ / ↓ denote their relation to the performance of the attacker:</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cards" data-augmented-ui="tl-clip br-clip border">
      <div class="card"><h4>Recall</h4><p>The baseline fraction of queries for which <i>R</i> retrieves a relevant image before attack.</p></div>
      <div class="card"><h4>ΔRecall ↓</h4><p>The change in the Recall of <i>R</i> after the attack, compared to the baseline Recall.</p></div>
      <div class="card"><h4>ASR-R ↑</h4><p>The fraction of targeted queries for which the malicious image is retrieved by <i>R</i>.</p></div>
    </div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>And the following performance metrics for generation:</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cards" data-augmented-ui="tl-clip br-clip border">
      <div class="card"><h4>ASR-G<sub>Sim</sub> ↑</h4><p>The average embedding similarity between the VLM generated response for targeted queries and the target response. ASR-G<sub>Sim</sub> = 1 means that the VLM outputs the target answer verbatim.</p></div>
      <div class="card"><h4>SIM-G<sub>Neg</sub> ↓</h4><p>In the targeted setting, the average embedding similarity between the VLM-generated response for non-targeted queries and the target malicious response.</p></div>
      <div class="card"><h4>SIM-G<sub>GT</sub> ↓</h4><p>The average embedding similarity between the VLM generated response for targeted queries and the ground truth response.</p></div>
    </div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>For all evaluation metrics, the superscript @<i>k</i> signifies that the metric is reported when the top-<i>k</i> relevant images are retrieved. Furthermore, we denote by @−1 that we force the malicious image to be retrieved as the only context image, which decouples the generation performance from retrieval, so we can report on the performance of each component of the attack individually. Similarity metrics are computed using the Jina-Embeddings-V3 text embedding model. We use an embedding model different from those employed by the VD-RAG system, and the attacks, to exclude the possibility of bias.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Attack Hyper-parameters</h3>
    <div class="table-wrap"><table class="data-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Hyper-parameter</th><th>Value</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Starting image <i>I′</i><sub>0</sub></td><td>An arbitrary image that is not relevant to any query</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Repeats</td><td>Five different initial images per attack</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Gradient steps</td><td>250, linear learning rate schedule from 3×10⁻³ to 3×10⁻⁴</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Batch size</td><td>8 user queries</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Loss weights</td><td>λ<sub>R</sub> = 2, λ<sub>G</sub> = 1</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Maximum image perturbation α</td><td>8/255</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Similarity in <i>L</i><sub>R</sub></td><td>Cosine similarity (MaxSim for ColPali)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Compute</td><td>NVIDIA H100 NVL GPU with 93GiB VRAM, approximately 325 GPU hours</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table></div>
    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:20px">
      <p>We observe that attacks can almost perfectly satisfy both the retrieval and the generation conditions starting from α = 8/255. Therefore, in the rest of the paper, we consider only attacks with α = 8/255. Despite the success of the attack, the perturbed image is almost indistinguishable from the original.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="psec" id="targeted">
    <h2>Targeted Attack</h2>
    <div class="stats op-quad">
      <div class="stat"><b class="red">1.00</b><span>ASR-R@1 when CLIP&#8209;L is the embedding model (white-box, Setting I)</span></div>
      <div class="stat"><b>0</b><span>false positive rate across all settings</span></div>
      <div class="stat"><b>1.00</b><span>white-box ASR-R@1 for every model pair on ViDoRe-V2-ESG</span></div>
      <div class="stat"><b>0.00</b><span>ASR-R@1 of Complete Transfer and Out-set Model Ensemble</span></div>
    </div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>We empirically evaluate the vulnerability of VD-RAG to the targeted attack across three settings, with increasing number of targeted queries and answers: I) One Query, II) Multiple Queries, and III) Multiple Queries &amp; Answers. Across all settings, the malicious image is never retrieved for unrelated queries, so the false positive rate (FPR) is always 0.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Setting I: One Query</h3>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>In this setting, we target a single query in the dataset such that the malicious KB entry is retrieved and it induces the VLM to generate a specified malicious answer generated by GPT-4o.</p>
      <p>Focusing on the white-box setting, the results show that an attacker with full knowledge of the target models can succeed in compromising the RAG system. For retrieval, the malicious image is always retrieved as the most relevant image for the target query when CLIP-L is the embedding model. For more sophisticated embedding models (i.e., ColPali and GME), the malicious image is almost always retrieved within the top-5 most relevant images. For generation, the generated answer is semantically similar to the target answer (ASR-G<sub>Sim</sub>@−1 ≥ 0.8) for most model combinations. Note that ASR-G<sub>Sim</sub>@−1 is relatively lower when ColPali or GME is used, as the attack optimization is not able to balance the retrieval and generation objectives. Further note that the malicious image has high specificity and does not influence the generated answers for untargeted queries, even when retrieved, as shown by the SIM-G<sub>Neg</sub>@−1 values.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="op-duo">
      <figure class="figure chart" data-augmented-ui="tl-clip br-clip border">
