  <section class="psec" id="universal">
    <h2>Universal Attack</h2>
    <div class="stats op-quad">
      <div class="stat"><b class="red">0.90–0.98</b><span>white-box ASR-R@1 with CLIP&#8209;L</span></div>
      <div class="stat"><b>0.00</b><span>white-box ASR-R@1 with ColPali and GME</span></div>
      <div class="stat"><b>8 of 9</b><span>white-box model pairs with ASR-G<sub>Sim</sub>@−1 of 0.87 or more</span></div>
      <div class="stat"><b>−0.19</b><span>largest ΔRecall@1, for CLIP&#8209;L (baseline 0.21)</span></div>
    </div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>Focusing on the white-box attack, the universal attack produces images that are always retrieved for all queries (ASR-R@1 = 1) when the CLIP-L embedding model is used. To the contrary, state-of-the-art embedding models (ColPali-v1.3 and GME-Qwen2-VL-2B) never retrieve adversarial images as the top-1 relevant image but sometimes retrieve them within the top-5. Regarding generation, the universal attack consistently causes all VLMs to generate the target answer verbatim for almost all user queries in the test dataset. These results mirror those for the targeted attacks, where CLIP-L is the most vulnerable embedding model, while ColPali and GME remain robust to influences under all attacks.</p>
    </div>

